## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while (_running)` + `Thread.Abort` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue execution | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |

### Why This Must Change

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. The fundamental shift is from **preemptive** (runtime injects exception) to **cooperative** (code voluntarily checks for cancellation).

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — replaces ThreadAbortException catch
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed; we simply continue to exit the loop
                // naturally since the while condition will be false.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for clean exit
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key Changes to `GetNextItem`

If `GetNextItem()` blocks (e.g., on a queue), it **must** accept and respect the token:

```csharp
// Before: blocks indefinitely, only Thread.Abort could break it
private WorkItem GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// After: throws OperationCanceledException when token is signalled
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
```

If using `Thread.Sleep` for polling:
```csharp
// Before
Thread.Sleep(1000);

// After — wakes immediately on cancellation
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1));
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** (`GetNextItem`, I/O) | Cancellation never takes effect until the call returns naturally — your thread hangs |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Always `Dispose()` it |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only fires at the next checkpoint. Long `ProcessItem` calls need internal `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` calls |
| **Replacing `Thread.Abort` with `Thread.Interrupt`** | Also obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9). Use `CancellationToken` |
| **Forgetting the `finally` block still runs** | With cooperative cancellation, `finally` runs normally — no `ResetAbort` gymnastics needed |

### Verification Checklist

- ✅ No `Thread.Abort`, `ThreadAbortException`, or `ResetAbort` references remain
- ✅ `CancellationToken` threaded through the full call chain
- ✅ Blocking calls accept the token
- ✅ `CancellationTokenSource` is disposed
- ✅ Builds with zero `SYSLIB0006` warnings on `net8.0`
